# OpenPete - Spyro the Dragon PC port (@VERSION@, @PLATFORM@)

OpenPete plays Spyro the Dragon (PSX, 1998) natively on your PC: widescreen,
high framerate, supersampling, HD texture packs and mods, with gameplay state
bit-identical to original PlayStation hardware.

You supply the disc. OpenPete ships no game content. On first run it copies
your disc image into its own library and compiles the game's code for your
machine: about 26 seconds on a 16-thread desktop, 45 to 90 seconds on a
four-core laptop. After that the original image is never needed again and the
game starts straight into play. Installing a new OpenPete release recompiles
once more (12 to 50 seconds), then starts fast again.

The full documentation ships with this build: open `docs/index.html` in a
browser. It is also at https://openpete.com/docs/.
<!-- @linux -->
In the AppImage it sits inside the read-only image: run
`./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract` once and open
`squashfs-root/usr/docs/index.html`.
<!-- @end -->

## Requirements

- Your own Spyro the Dragon (NTSC-U) disc, dumped to a `.bin`/`.cue` pair.
- A 64-bit x86-64 machine with a GPU driver supporting Vulkan 1.2.
- About 700 MB of disk: the library keeps its own copy of the disc image
  (~640 MB, one copy per distinct disc) plus ~26 MB per compiled build.
<!-- @linux -->
- glibc 2.31 or newer (Debian 11, Ubuntu 20.04, or later).
<!-- @end -->

## Quick start

1. Dump your disc to a `.bin`/`.cue` pair.
<!-- @windows -->
2. Run `openpete-spyro1.exe`.
3. Drag your `.bin` or `.cue` onto the window, or press Space (or A on a pad)
   to browse for it. You can also pass the path on the command line
   (`openpete-spyro1.exe mydisc.cue`) or open the image from Explorer with
   "Open with".
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
2. Make it executable and run it: `chmod +x OpenPete-*.AppImage` then
   `./OpenPete-*.AppImage` for the AppImage, or `./openpete-spyro1` from the
   extracted `.tar.xz`.
3. Drag your `.bin` or `.cue` onto the window, or press Space (or A on a pad)
   to browse for it. You can also pass the path on the command line
   (`./OpenPete-*.AppImage mydisc.cue`) or use your file manager's "Open with".
<!-- @end -->

## Controls

Keyboard defaults for the PlayStation pad:

| Pad | Key | Pad | Key |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cross | C | Circle | V |
| Square | X | Triangle | Z |
| D-pad | arrow keys | Left stick | W A S D |
| Start | Return | Select | Space |
| L1 / L2 / L3 | Left Shift / Left Ctrl / `[` | R1 / R2 / R3 | Right Shift / Right Ctrl / `]` |

Any connected gamepad plays the game with its own buttons; no keyboard is
needed.

OpenPete's own keys:

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Escape | open/close the settings menu |
| Alt+Enter | toggle fullscreen |
| Tab | hold to fast-forward |
| F7 / F8 | pause / advance one tick while paused |
| 1 / 2 / 3 | savestate: save, load, cycle the active slot |
| Backspace | hold to rewind (only with `--rewind N`) |
| F | free camera; T drops Spyro at it |
| I / L | toggle interpolation / extended sector detail |
| M | dev config overlay |

Every action above rebinds under `[keys.pad]` and `[keys.host]` in
`openpete.toml`; `--keys-report` prints every action, its binding, and where
that binding came from.

## Settings

The settings menu (Escape) covers windowed/fullscreen, aspect, FPS target,
vsync, supersampling, vertex wobble, texture mapping, dither, sector LOD, free
camera, and the enabled mods; its SETTINGS row writes your choices into
`openpete.toml`, and EXIT GAME quits. This build ships an `openpete.toml`
holding one line, `fullscreen = true`.

Everything else is a command-line flag or an `openpete.toml` key of the same
name. `openpete-spyro1 --help` lists the flags; `--config-report` prints which
setting won and what it overrode.

## Where your files go

<!-- @windows -->
Windows keeps everything in the program's own folder, next to
`openpete-spyro1.exe`:

```
openpete.toml         your settings
gamecontrollerdb.txt  controller mappings (see below)
cards/                memory cards (the game's own saves)
states/               savestate slots
logs/                 openpete.log - attach to a bug report
library/              your ingested disc and its compiled code
mods/                 drop-in mods
assets/               your own texture packs, sounds and music
bk2/                  input recordings
docs/                 the documentation: open docs/index.html
sdk/                  mod SDK: headers, examples/ (only when packaged with it)
recomp/               the bundled compiler and recompiler
```
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
OpenPete follows the XDG directories, so nothing is written next to the
program itself:

```
~/.config/openpete/openpete.toml          your settings
~/.config/openpete/gamecontrollerdb.txt   your controller mappings (see below)
~/.local/state/openpete/cards/     memory cards (the game's own saves)
~/.local/state/openpete/states/    savestate slots
~/.local/state/openpete/logs/      openpete.log - attach to a bug report
~/.local/share/openpete/library/   your ingested disc and its compiled code
~/.local/share/openpete/mods/      drop-in mods
~/.local/share/openpete/assets/    your own texture packs, sounds and music
~/.local/share/openpete/bk2/       input recordings
```

Set `portable = true` at the top of `openpete.toml` to keep all of them in the
program's own folder instead. That works for the `.tar.xz`; the AppImage is a
read-only file, so it always uses the paths above.
<!-- @end -->

## Controllers

Most pads work with no setup. If yours is not recognised, or its buttons are
in the wrong places, you can add a mapping without touching anything that
ships with the game.

<!-- @windows -->
Edit `gamecontrollerdb.txt` next to `openpete-spyro1.exe` and add a line for
your pad. Keep a copy of that line somewhere else: extracting a new release
over this folder replaces the file.
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
Create `~/.config/openpete/gamecontrollerdb.txt` and put a line for your pad
in it. One line is enough: it is merged with the mappings that ship with the
game, and yours wins wherever both describe the same pad. It also survives
upgrades, unlike the copy inside the AppImage or the tarball.
<!-- @end -->

Mappings come from https://github.com/mdqinc/SDL_GameControllerDB, which
documents how to generate a line for a pad that has none. Two other routes
work if you already use them: `SDL_GAMECONTROLLERCONFIG_FILE` names a mapping
file to load instead of the one that ships here, and `--controller-db PATH`
loads one more file on top of everything above.

`openpete.log` records every mapping file that was looked for, and for each
pad the mapping that was actually used and where it came from.

## Notes

<!-- @linux -->
- The AppImage carries its own compiler, so first run needs nothing installed.
  The `.tar.xz` uses your system `cc` unless it was packaged with a bundled one
  (`sudo apt install gcc` or equivalent).
- If the AppImage exits with a FUSE error, your distribution no longer installs
  libfuse by default. Either install it, or run the image without it:
  `./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
- To get OpenPete into your application menu, either install `appimaged`/Gear
  Lever, or copy `com.openpete.OpenPete.desktop` and
  `com.openpete.OpenPete.png` out of `./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract`
  into `~/.local/share/applications` and `~/.local/share/icons` and point
  `Exec=` at where you keep the image.
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @windows -->
- An unsigned download makes Windows show a SmartScreen warning ("Windows
  protected your PC"): click "More info", then "Run anyway". If in doubt,
  verify the download against the published `.sha256` checksum first.
- Install to a reasonably short path (for example `C:\Games\OpenPete`); very
  deep folders can hit Windows' path-length limit during first run.
<!-- @end -->

## Mods

A mod is a folder (or a `.zip`) dropped into the `mods` directory above,
holding a `mod.toml` manifest plus any of `assets/` (replacement textures,
music, sounds, custom levels) and `src/*.c`. Source mods are compiled on the
next start against the `sdk` folder next to the program, by the same compiler
that built your disc's module; a start that has mods to compile shows
"COMPILING MODS" on the splash. If there is no `sdk` folder next to the
program, this build was packaged without the mod SDK: source mods are
disabled, and mods made only of a manifest and assets still load.
`mods/enabled.txt` (one mod ID per line) fixes which mods load and in what
order; without it every mod loads, alphabetically.

A source mod runs inside the game process with the same privileges as the
game. There is no sandbox. Install only mods you trust, as you would any other
program. A mod with no `src/` directory never loads code.

Mod authors: when this build carries the SDK, `docs/index.html` includes the
Modding section (start at "Getting started") and `sdk/examples/` holds
working example mods. Copy one into the `mods` directory above to run it;
nothing under `sdk/` loads on its own. In the AppImage both sit inside the
read-only image: run `./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract` once and open
`squashfs-root/usr/docs/index.html` and `squashfs-root/usr/sdk/examples/`.
Without the SDK, the Modding section is at https://openpete.com/docs/modding/.

## Reporting a problem

Every run mirrors its diagnostics to `openpete.log` in the `logs` directory
above: the startup sequence, what it decided about your disc and your
hardware, any driver errors, and the crash dump if it crashed. Attach it. The
previous run is kept alongside it as `openpete.log.1`, which is the one to
attach when a relaunch made the problem go away.

Nothing in the log identifies you beyond the paths OpenPete itself uses.

